WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:03.300
-You know our first guest
from his work in

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.267
"Game of Thrones," "Narcos," and
the hit series "The Last Of Us,"

00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:08.934
which airs new episodes Sunday
nights on HBO and HBO Max.

00:00:08.934 --> 00:00:10.701
He also stars in
"The Mandalorian,"

00:00:10.701 --> 00:00:12.200
which is available on Disney+.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.200
The highly anticipated
third season

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.701
will premiere on March 1st.

00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:17.300
Let's take a look.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.567
-Now, as I was saying,
there's a beautiful parcel

00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:22.667
available right down here
by the Flats.

00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:27.868
-I appreciate the offer, but I
have some matters to look after.

00:00:27.868 --> 00:00:29.100
-Well, I'm confused.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.767
I thought you had
completed your mission,

00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:32.734
but you're still
running around here

00:00:32.734 --> 00:00:36.067
with the same little critter.

00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:39.334
-It's complicated.

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:43.067
I completed my quest.
He returned to me.

00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:47.133
I removed my helmet
and now I'm an apostate.

00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:50.834
-Which is all the more reason
for you to stay here with us.

00:00:52.901 --> 00:00:55.300
Where you're from,
you may be an apostate,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.300
but here,
you'd be landed gentry.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.133
-Please welcome back
to the show our friend

00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:02.200
Pedro Pascal, everybody!

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.601
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:20.734
I'm so happy you're here.

00:01:20.734 --> 00:01:22.033
-I'm so glad to be back.

00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:24.701
-And I feel like, you know,
to get an actual clip

00:01:24.701 --> 00:01:27.267
from "The Mandalorian"
is a big deal.

00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:29.534
I take that as a real gift to us
and our show

00:01:29.534 --> 00:01:30.601
that you came with a clip.

00:01:30.601 --> 00:01:32.067
-Who did you kill
or who's going to die?

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:34.200
-I know.
Somebody is going to die.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.033
You were not supposed to
show that to us.

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:38.167
We talked in the past

00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:39.400
We're, you know,
about the same age.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.734
Obviously, "Star Wars"
means a great deal to us.

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:42.000
Do you remember?

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.734
What was the first of the
"Star Wars" you saw?

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:48.133
-There had to have been a
re-release of the first one...

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:49.501
-Yeah, I think that's right.

00:01:49.501 --> 00:01:51.167
-...because I fell like
I saw it in the movie theater.

00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:54.067
I was traumatized by
the trash compactor.

00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:55.767
-Yeah. I still won't go in one.

00:01:55.767 --> 00:01:57.601
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.601 --> 00:01:58.734
I remember, in high school,

00:01:58.734 --> 00:01:59.767
my friends were going down
to the compactor.

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:00.968
I'm like, "Not me."

00:02:00.968 --> 00:02:02.501
-You're like,
"I'm not doing that.

00:02:02.501 --> 00:02:03.834
I saw 'Star Wars.'
Are you kidding?"

00:02:03.834 --> 00:02:06.100
-You kidding me?
At some point, they compact.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.734
That's the whole thing.

00:02:07.734 --> 00:02:09.033
-Beheaded Obi-Wan.

00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:12.133
You know, I remember
that traumatizing me, as well.

00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:14.834
But I distinctly remember
"Empire Strikes Back"

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.734
and finally getting tickets to
"Return of the Jedi."

00:02:17.734 --> 00:02:19.067
-Yeah.

00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:22.434
-Which, if I was being honest,
is my favorite one.

00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:24.167
-Yeah. I know.

00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:28.067
I'm glad you said that, because
I feel like I'm in this, too.

00:02:28.067 --> 00:02:29.467
I mean,
that was the biggest deal.

00:02:29.467 --> 00:02:31.601
Now, again,
it happened at the right time.

00:02:31.601 --> 00:02:35.267
Right. And everybody now says,
"Oh, you know, the Ewoks..."

00:02:35.267 --> 00:02:37.467
-I would say it was
because of the Ewoks.

00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:39.934
-I love the Ewoks.
I love the Ewoks.

00:02:39.934 --> 00:02:42.133
You know,
I was 11 years old or whatever.

00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:44.734
The Ewoks were exactly what
I was looking for in my cinema.

00:02:44.734 --> 00:02:46.234
-Exactly.

00:02:46.234 --> 00:02:48.601
-I don't want to apologize.
I feel like it's like "SNL."

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.200
It's like, you know,
the cast you grew up with.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.901
I'm like, "The Ewoks
are my favorite cast."

00:02:53.901 --> 00:02:55.968
[ Laughs ]

00:02:55.968 --> 00:02:59.267
And so it just must be so cool
to be a part of that world now.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:00.567
-It's crazy.

00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:02.467
It's so strange
because it's something that is

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:05.767
so imprinted in your childhood,
and now you're a part of it.

00:03:05.767 --> 00:03:10.934
And it is a very surreal
and big thing that I'm proud of.

00:03:10.934 --> 00:03:12.167
-It's really cool.

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:14.133
It's a lot of reasons
to be proud of it.

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:16.267
Now, obviously,
your parents let you see

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:17.367
the "Star Wars" movies,
as did mine.

00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:18.734
-Yeah.

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:20.667
-But there was a movie your dad
would not let you see,

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:22.701
also from an era
of our childhood.

00:03:22.701 --> 00:03:25.734
It's really funny this is
the movie he drew the line on.

00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:29.200
-Only because he didn't draw the
line on "The Big Chill

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.501
and "First Blood"
and stuff like that.

00:03:33.501 --> 00:03:34.934
-Which are -- Those are good.

00:03:34.934 --> 00:03:37.200
I can see why a parent
would draw the line on those.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.834
So, what did your dad
not want you to watch?

00:03:38.834 --> 00:03:41.634
-I was not allowed to see
"The Breakfast Club."

00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:44.968
-Okay.
-In the movie theater.

00:03:44.968 --> 00:03:46.801
-Now, no nudity in
"The Breakfast Club."

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:48.033
-There was no nudity.
-Yeah.

00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:50.100
So what was his --
What was his issue with it?

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.434
-He was like,
"These are a bunch of kids

00:03:51.434 --> 00:03:53.234
complaining about
their parents."

00:03:53.234 --> 00:03:54.534
-Oh.

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:58.601
-And he's like, "[Bleep] that."

00:03:58.601 --> 00:04:00.701
-Wow.

00:04:00.701 --> 00:04:02.601
I mean, did he --
How would he even know that?

00:04:02.601 --> 00:04:04.033
Did he go see it and he's like,

00:04:04.033 --> 00:04:06.467
"We cannot
let this get to Pedro"?

00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:08.701
-I don't think
he was pre-screening it.

00:04:08.701 --> 00:04:12.901
But I was absorbing quite a bit
of John Hughes at the time,

00:04:12.901 --> 00:04:15.567
as we all were, maybe.

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:19.934
"Sixteen Candles," which was PG.

00:04:19.934 --> 00:04:21.968
-Yeah. "Sixteen Candles,"
looking back,

00:04:21.968 --> 00:04:23.534
is maybe the one
that you're like,

00:04:23.534 --> 00:04:25.167
"Oh, I wish
I didn't show my kids that."

00:04:25.167 --> 00:04:26.167
-Exactly.

00:04:26.167 --> 00:04:27.868
-The messaging in
"Sixteen Candles"

00:04:27.868 --> 00:04:29.067
has not held up.

00:04:29.067 --> 00:04:31.100
-No. That was at
birthday parties, you know?

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:32.968
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:32.968 --> 00:04:35.033
-But "The Breakfast Club"
was rated R,

00:04:35.033 --> 00:04:38.734
and I wasn't allowed to see it
until it came out on VHS.

00:04:38.734 --> 00:04:40.300
And I still
wasn't allowed to see it,

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:43.167
because I was
on the pulse of this movie.

00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:44.868
I needed to see this movie.

00:04:44.868 --> 00:04:47.501
And he's like, "No."

00:04:47.501 --> 00:04:49.934
But then they couldn't find
a babysitter.

00:04:49.934 --> 00:04:55.734
And I was like 10.

00:04:55.734 --> 00:04:57.934
And my sister must have been 12.

00:04:57.934 --> 00:04:59.834
And they're like, "Uh, okay,

00:04:59.834 --> 00:05:02.000
you can watch
'The Breakfast Club.'

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.167
We're going rent it for you.

00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:04.667
Just put it in the VCR

00:05:04.667 --> 00:05:05.968
and then rewind it and start it
over and just keep watching it

00:05:05.968 --> 00:05:08.567
-That is such a wild swing
that they went from

00:05:08.567 --> 00:05:10.767
"You can't see it"
to they, like, effectively

00:05:10.767 --> 00:05:12.400
let it babysit their children.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.834
-Basically.
They're like -- Right, right.

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:18.367
-It's so funny, like, when you
think of the idea that parents

00:05:18.367 --> 00:05:21.267
think they can make a thing
you want to see contraband

00:05:21.267 --> 00:05:23.334
and you won't want to see it
like a hundred times more.

00:05:23.334 --> 00:05:26.067
Like, they did -- What marketing
for "Breakfast Club"

00:05:26.067 --> 00:05:28.934
that they were like,
"You will never."

00:05:28.934 --> 00:05:30.534
Yeah. And then how many times
do you think

00:05:30.534 --> 00:05:31.767
you ended up watching it?

00:05:31.767 --> 00:05:34.801
-I think I watched it
three times that night.

00:05:34.801 --> 00:05:37.200
-You just mainlined it.
-Even my sister was over it.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.200
She's like,
"We're not watching it again?"

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.534
"Oh, I am!"
You know?

00:05:41.534 --> 00:05:42.801
-I got a lot more questions.

00:05:42.801 --> 00:05:45.801
We'll be right back with more
from Pedro Pascal.

